new file mode 100644
index 000000000..f2852107c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/MULTI_GROUP_ROUTING_MAINTENANCE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+# Multi-Group Routing Patch Maintenance
+
+This patch branch is based on the official `v0.2.16` tag. The feature changes the API key schema, routing and billing context, Redis cooldown state, gateway failover loops, and the API key editor. It is source code intended for a custom image; editing a running container or generated frontend files is not a durable installation method.
+
+## Build a custom image
+
+From the repository root, build the frontend and backend together with the maintained patch branch:
+
+```powershell
+docker build -f deploy/Dockerfile --build-arg VERSION=v0.2.16-multi-group-routing -t bon2/sub2api:v0.2.16-multi-group-routing .
+```
+
+The image build embeds the frontend and server binary. The API key routing migration is embedded in the backend and is applied by the normal database migration startup path. Back up the database before the first deployment and verify container health plus an API request after the migration.
+
+## Request-boundary behavior
+
+The routing policy is applied only while a request can still be retried without
+duplicating a resource or joining an existing session. The implemented
+group-level failover covers text, embeddings, images, search, Gemini
+generation, and Grok TTS/STT before any response bytes are committed.
+
+The following operations deliberately stay bound to their original group or
+session and do not cross groups: Grok media generation and video
+status/content lookups, Gemini explicit-cache lifecycle calls and requests
+that reference an existing cached content, Grok custom-voices operations,
+OpenAI Live, and OpenAI/Grok Realtime WebSocket sessions. These paths
+currently do not perform API-key group-level failover. A pre-handshake failure
+may still be handled by endpoint-level account failover, but the request is
+not replayed as a different API-key group once a session or resource may have
+been accepted.
+
+## Upgrade the patch to a newer upstream version
+
+An upstream image update will not contain local changes unless upstream has merged this feature. To maintain the patch:
+
+1. Commit the reviewed feature changes on `feature/multi-group-routing`.
+2. Fetch the next official source tag and create an upgrade branch from that tag.
+3. Rebase or cherry-pick the feature commit(s) onto the upgrade branch and resolve conflicts in Ent-generated code, migrations, and gateway handlers.
+4. Regenerate Ent code if schemas changed, then run backend tests, frontend type checks/build, and the migration checks.
+5. Build and deploy the new custom image; keep the previous image tag and a database backup available for rollback.
+
+For example, once the patch has been committed and upstream publishes `v0.2.17`:
+
+```powershell
+git fetch upstream --tags
+git switch -c upgrade/v0.2.17 v0.2.17
+git cherry-pick <multi-group-routing-commit>
+docker build -f deploy/Dockerfile --build-arg VERSION=v0.2.17-multi-group-routing -t bon2/sub2api:v0.2.17-multi-group-routing .
+```
+
+If a future upstream version includes the feature, compare its behavior and migration history, then drop the local patch instead of carrying duplicate changes. Until then, each new upstream version needs a source-level rebase/merge and regression run; it does not require manually copying production artifacts.
+
+## Rollback
+
+Rollback the application by deploying the previous image tag. Migration `246_api_key_routes.sql` is additive: the old application can continue using the legacy `api_keys.group_id` projection while the route table and routing flag remain in the database. Do not remove the route table or column as part of an image rollback; take and retain a database backup before the first feature deployment.
